      "content": "The labor movement's gains were worth the short-term pain because they permanently lowered the cost of living for everyone by capping corporate profits and raising wages. While some critics claim unions hurt productivity, history shows that organized workers actually drove innovation and economic stability by preventing the exploitative practices that once drove down wages and up prices. The alternative—a society where corporations could dictate terms without consequence—would have resulted in even higher inequality and less freedom for ordinary people.",
